# The AMD GPU architectures both backends are built for, one per line.
#
# Read by scripts/windows/build-hip-dll.ps1 for avian_amd.dll and by
# scripts/release.sh for libavian_amd.so, so the two platforms cover the same
# hardware. Edit here, not in either script.
#
# Unlike CUDA there is no PTX to fall back on: a card not listed here will not
# launch the result at all. So the list errs towards covering consumer
# hardware rather than towards a quick build. Build time scales with it, at
# roughly 1.9 minutes and 2.3 MB of library per target.

# RDNA2, RX 6000.
gfx1030
# The smaller RDNA2 parts, RX 6700 and RX 6600. AMD's own Windows support for
# them has been going backwards: the runtime row is a tick on the 5.6 through
# 7.1.1 pages and a cross on 7.2.0, while the HIP SDK row is a cross
# throughout. Building for them costs one pass each and makes the failure, if
# it comes, a clear message from the runtime rather than a kernel that
# silently will not launch.
gfx1031
gfx1032
# Van Gogh and the Rembrandt and Raphael APUs. Slow wherever they work; the
# value in having them is a clear "ran correctly" rather than throughput.
gfx1033
gfx1034
gfx1035
gfx1036
# RDNA3, RX 7000.
gfx1100
gfx1101
gfx1102
# Strix and Krackan. Added only once gfx1030 had hashed correctly on real
# hardware, so that a failure on one of these is that part's failure rather
# than a question about the whole port.
gfx1150
gfx1151
gfx1152
gfx1153
# RDNA4, RX 9000.
gfx1200
gfx1201
